2025-05-01 03:16:11 - Thread-1275 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and their possible use  

payment  
• "customer_id": ties every payment to a customer.  
• "amount": the money paid in each transaction – the thing whose month‑to‑month change we need.  
• "payment_date": tells us the exact day/time of the payment; we will truncate it to month.  

customer  
• "customer_id": primary key that joins to payment.customer_id.  
• "first_name", "last_name": needed for the requested full name in the final answer.  

Typical conditions we will use  
• Join payment → customer on the shared customer_id.  
• Use strftime('%Y-%m', "payment_date") to turn a timestamp into the month string ‘YYYY‑MM’.  
• Window function lag() to look at the previous month’s total and compute month‑to‑month change.  
• Aggregate (AVG) the absolute month‑to‑month change for every customer and pick the greatest one.

--------------------------------------------------
Below are 10 exploratory / step‑by‑step SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows) that go from simple inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1) Look at some raw rows in payment
SELECT *
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Which distinct customers have made any payment?
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Show the names of the first 20 customers who appear in payment
SELECT DISTINCT c."customer_id",
       c."first_name" || ' ' || c."last_name" AS "full_name"
FROM   "customer"  AS c
JOIN   "payment"   AS p  USING ("customer_id")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) For one sample customer, list payments with the month extracted
SELECT p."customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
       p."amount"
FROM   "payment" AS p
WHERE  p."customer_id" = 1
ORDER BY p."payment_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Total amount per customer per month (first 20 rows)
SELECT p."customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
       SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
FROM   "payment" AS p
GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
ORDER  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Same as (5) but now include previous month total to see changes
SELECT t.*,
       LAG(t."month_total") OVER (PARTITION BY t."customer_id"
                                  ORDER BY t."ym") AS "prev_month_total"
FROM (
      SELECT p."customer_id",
             strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
             SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
      FROM   "payment" AS p
      GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
) AS t
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Add the absolute change month‑to‑month per customer
SELECT t."customer_id",
       t."ym",
       t."month_total",
       ABS(t."month_total" -
           LAG(t."month_total") OVER (PARTITION BY t."customer_id"
                                      ORDER BY t."ym")) AS "change_from_prev"
FROM (
      SELECT p."customer_id",
             strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
             SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
      FROM   "payment" AS p
      GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
) AS t
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Average monthly change for each customer (first 20)
WITH month_changes AS (
    SELECT t."customer_id",
           ABS(t."month_total" -
               LAG(t."month_total") OVER (PARTITION BY t."customer_id"
                                          ORDER BY t."ym")) AS "chg"
    FROM (
          SELECT p."customer_id",
                 strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
                 SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
          FROM   "payment" AS p
          GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
    ) AS t
)
SELECT "customer_id",
       AVG("chg") AS "avg_monthly_change"
FROM   month_changes
WHERE  "chg" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY "customer_id"
ORDER  BY "avg_monthly_change" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Identify the customer_id with the single highest average monthly change
WITH month_changes AS (
    SELECT t."customer_id",
           ABS(t."month_total" -
               LAG(t."month_total") OVER (PARTITION BY t."customer_id"
                                          ORDER BY t."ym")) AS "chg"
    FROM (
          SELECT p."customer_id",
                 strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
                 SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
          FROM   "payment" AS p
          GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
    ) AS t
),
avg_change AS (
    SELECT "customer_id",
           AVG("chg") AS "avg_monthly_change"
    FROM   month_changes
    WHERE  "chg" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP  BY "customer_id"
)
SELECT *
FROM   avg_change
ORDER  BY "avg_monthly_change" DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final answer: full name of that top customer
WITH month_changes AS (
    SELECT t."customer_id",
           ABS(t."month_total" -
               LAG(t."month_total") OVER (PARTITION BY t."customer_id"
                                          ORDER BY t."ym")) AS "chg"
    FROM (
          SELECT p."customer_id",
                 strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "ym",
                 SUM(p."amount") AS "month_total"
          FROM   "payment" AS p
          GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "ym"
    ) AS t
),
avg_change AS (
    SELECT "customer_id",
           AVG("chg") AS "avg_monthly_change"
    FROM   month_changes
    WHERE  "chg" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP  BY "customer_id"
),
top_cust AS (
    SELECT "customer_id"
    FROM   avg_change
    ORDER  BY "avg_monthly_change" DESC
    LIMIT 1
)
SELECT c."first_name" || ' ' || c."last_name" AS "full_name"
FROM   "customer" AS c
JOIN   top_cust      USING ("customer_id");
```

Running the last query will return exactly one row: the full name of the customer who exhibits the highest average month‑to‑month change in total payment amounts.
